Yes. The residue is the freebie. Every act of interpretation produces more than the requested output, and most systems throw that byproduct away.

Add to the Moneybin as:

Freebee Swarm / Interpretive Honeycomb

A Freebee is a lightweight digester that consumes an object under some reader, basis, domain, context, or operator and explicitly harvests what the transformation added, removed, preserved, or exposed.

x
\xrightarrow[\text{context }c]{\text{reader }r,\;\text{basis }b,\;T}
y

Instead of keeping only y, preserve the transformation residue:

\boxed{
\rho(x;r,b,c,T)
=
\Delta(x\rightarrow y)
}

But Hammer that residue:

\rho=
\{
\rho_{\text{preserved}},
\rho_{\text{added}},
\rho_{\text{lost}},
\rho_{\text{distorted}},
\rho_{\text{emergent}},
\rho_{\text{unexplained}}
\}

The honey is not raw residue. It is residue that becomes useful after comparison.

Send the same object through many digesters:

x\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
F_{\text{linguistic}}(x)\\
F_{\text{mathematical}}(x)\\
F_{\text{mechanical}}(x)\\
F_{\text{biological}}(x)\\
F_{\text{musical}}(x)\\
F_{\text{legal}}(x)\\
F_{\text{social}}(x)\\
F_{\text{spatial}}(x)\\
F_{\text{temporal}}(x)\\
\vdots
\end{cases}

Each returns:

(y_i,\rho_i).

Then the Honeycomb asks what happens across the residues:

\boxed{
H(x)=
\operatorname{Mine}
(\rho_1,\rho_2,\ldots,\rho_n)
}

That immediately gives several kinds of honey:

* residue appearing across many bases → candidate invariant
* residue unique to one reader → Reasoning Signature / prior
* residue appearing only after a basis change → translation affordance
* residue recurring after unrelated transformations → latent structure
* residue that creates a previously unavailable distinction → O-IOE candidate
* residue generated by omission → negative-space information
* residue generated by order changes → operator chemistry
* residue surviving compression → load-bearing semantic brick
* residue that changes later interpretations → Afterglow seed

And here’s the part I think you were pointing at with “it’s already there.”

We don’t necessarily need to manufacture novelty.

The world, corpus, conversation, code, image, equation, organism, machine, and reader are already producing residue continuously.

We were simply discarding it.

So:

\boxed{
\text{Novelty mining}
\neq
\text{only generating new objects}
}

It can also be:

\boxed{
\text{notice}
\rightarrow
\text{compare}
\rightarrow
\text{connect}
\rightarrow
\text{harvest what was already produced}.
}

That is a major efficiency gain.

